<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna re-argument-recovery-drops-both: a call inside a loop renders with an
    empty argument list while the two instructions before it plainly load its
    argument registers, and the very same function recovers arguments fine at a
    call that is not in that loop.

    Funcdata::onlyOpUse (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) walks every descendant of the
    Varnode reaching a CALL in a candidate argument register and rejects the
    trial when another CALL also consumes it.  The x86 register-clearing idiom
    puts a fake such consumer on every loop: the value passed at the bottom of
    the body reaches the next iteration's `xor esi,esi` through the
    killed-by-call INDIRECT, the loop-head MULTIEQUAL and the SUBPIECE that
    slices esi out of rsi, and from there the call that follows the clear, whose
    own trial for that register is active.  INT_XOR(v,v) is 0 whatever v is, so
    that use cannot observe the value at all.  The operands are still two
    distinct SUBPIECE Varnodes here, so the sameness test has to be structural.

    The vector is sub_113d9 of WeeperVM Level 1 (challenge
    67f9bdc38f555589f3530a85), a stripped x86-64 ELF whose per-input-byte
    watchdog(timers, count) call at 0x11477 renders watchdog(), reduced to one
    loop and assembled from
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/zeroidiomuse_x86_64.s:

        401068  44 39 e3        CMP   EBX,R12D      ; loop head
        40106b  7e 20           JLE   0x40108d
        40106d  31 f6           XOR   ESI,ESI       ; the fake competing use
        40106f  31 ff           XOR   EDI,EDI
        401071  e8 8a ff ff ff  CALL  reader        ; its trials are active
        401076  be 02 00 00 00  MOV   ESI,0x2
        40107b  bf 00 20 40 00  MOV   EDI,0x402000
        401080  41 ff c4        INC   R12D
        401083  e8 88 ff ff ff  CALL  watchdog      ; both trials sink
        401088  48 85 c0        TEST  RAX,RAX
        40108b  74 db           JZ    0x401068

    watchdog itself pushes rcx and calls out before its loop, which is what
    keeps the calleearity family from reconstructing the list from the callee
    body instead.

    Phase: P4 active input-trial scoring, option zeroidiomuse off|on.  Pass 1
    (off) pins the bug: watchdog() with no arguments.  Pass 2 (on, the shipped
    default) pins the recovered pair.  Nothing else in the function moves.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000">

</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x402000">
00000000000000000000000000000000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="reader"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401010" name="watchdog"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401050" name="now"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401060" name="feed"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x402000" name="timers"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option zeroidiomuse off</com>
  <com>lo fu feed</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option zeroidiomuse on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="ZEROIDIOMUSE #1: off, the watchdog call inside the loop has an empty argument list" min="1" max="1">watchdog\(\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ZEROIDIOMUSE #2: on, both loaded registers reach the call as arguments" min="1" max="1">watchdog\(timers,2\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ZEROIDIOMUSE #3: the zeroing idiom itself is folded to the constants reader is called with in BOTH passes, so the option moved no other call" min="2" max="2">reader\(0,0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ZEROIDIOMUSE #4: both passes keep the loop counter and its two returns, so only the argument list moved" min="4" max="4">return v[0-9]+;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
